   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,041:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: | Neural | ~474 |
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,041:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,041:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: |
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,042:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: DFT (medium) | ~818 |
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,045:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Latency is stable across Eb/N0 for all methods. LS, PSO, Adaptive, ISTA, and Neural all cluster around 470-480
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,045:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: ms. DFT is 1.7x slower due to the medium factory configuration and DFT processing overhead.
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,045:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 3.3 Runtime
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,046:      pdf_renderer.py:  235: /app/reports/plots/estimator_runtime.png, 1308, 713
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,054:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: | Method | Total Runtime | Relative to LS |
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,055:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,055:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: |---|---|---|
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,055:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,055:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: | ISTA | 91.7 s | 0.11x |
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,055:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,056:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: | DFT | 96.5 s | 0.12x |
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,056:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,056:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: | LS | 817.7 s |
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,056:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 1.0x |
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,056:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,057:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: | Neural | 817.4 s | 1.0x |
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,057:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,057:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: | Adaptive | 5,838 s | 7.1x |
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,057:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,057:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: | PSO | 82,703 s (23.0 h) | 101x |
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,058:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: l
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,058:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: ISTA and DFT are fastest
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,059:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  (~92-97 s), about 9x faster than LS due to fewer Monte Carlo batches
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,059:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: needed.
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,059:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: l
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,059:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Adaptive is 7x slower than LS
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,060:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  but delivers 10x better BER -- a strong cost-benefit tradeoff.
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,060:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: l
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,060:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: PSO is 101x slower than LS
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,062:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  for marginal improvement over Adaptive. The particle swarm
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,062:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: optimization explores many candidate solutions per batch, making it computationally expensive. 
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,063:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Not recommended for production use.
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,063:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: l
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,063:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: LS and Neural have identical runtime
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,064:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: , confirming functional equivalence.
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,064:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 4. Modulation Order Impact
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,065:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 4.1 BER vs Modulation
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,065:      pdf_renderer.py:  235: /app/reports/plots/modulation_ber_vs_ebno.png, 1295, 829
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,076:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: | Eb/N0 (dB) | QPSK | 16-QAM | 64-QAM |
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,076:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,076:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: |---|---|---|---|
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,077:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,077:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: | 0  | 2.42e-2 | 1.39e-1 | 2.31e-1 |
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,077:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,077:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: | 4  | 9.65e-4 | 1.14e-2 |
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,078:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 6.83e-2 |
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,078:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,078:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: | 8  | 3.46e-4 | 2.49e-3 | 1.43e-2 |
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,078:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,078:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: | 12 | 2.82e-4 | 1.72e-3 | 8.13e-3 |
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,079:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,079:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: | 16 | 3.22e-4 | 1.66e-3 | 8.85e-3 |
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,079:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,079:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: | 20 |
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,080:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 4.00e-4 | 1.45e-3 | 1.11e-2 |
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,080:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: All tests use the LS estimator on the TR 38.901 UMi channel.
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,080:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Analysis:
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,080:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: l
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,081:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: BER floor scales with modulation order
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,084:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: : QPSK ~3 x 10^-4, 16-QAM ~1.5 x 10^-3 (5x worse),
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,086:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 64-QAM ~1.1 x 10^-2 (37x worse). This is expected -- higher-order constellations have smaller
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,086:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: decision regions and are more sensitive to estimation errors.
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,087:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: l
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,087:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 64-QAM BER increases at high SNR
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,087:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  (from 8.1e-3 at 12 dB to 1.1e-2 at 20 dB), indicating the LS
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,088:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: estimator
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,088:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,088:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: s channel estimation error floor is especially damaging for dense constellations.
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,088:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: l
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,089:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: To use higher-order modulation effectively, a better estimator (Adaptive or PSO) would be needed.
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,089:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 4.2 Latency vs Modulation
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,090:      pdf_renderer.py:  235: /app/reports/plots/modulation_latency_vs_ebno.png, 1299, 829
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,100:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: | Modulation | Avg. Latency (ms) | Throughput (bits/batch) |
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,100:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,100:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: |---|---|---|
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,100:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,101:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: | QPSK (2 bits/sym) | 471 | ~393k |
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,101:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,101:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: | 16-QAM
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,102:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: (4 bits/sym) | 774 | ~785k |
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,102:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,102:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: | 64-QAM (6 bits/sym) | 1,690 | ~1.17M |
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,105:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Latency scales roughly linearly with bits per symbol due to increased LDPC decoding complexity. Raw throughput
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,105:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: increases, but the higher BER means more retransmissions would be needed in practice.
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,106:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 5. Channel Model Impact
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,106:      pdf_renderer.py:  235: /app/reports/plots/channel_model_ber_vs_ebno.png, 1295, 829
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,117:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: | Eb/N0 (dB) | Rayleigh | Rician (K=1) | TR 38.901 UMi |
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,117:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,117:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: |---|---|---|---|
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,117:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,118:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: | 0  | 7.47e-3 | 9.17e-2 | 2.49e-2 |
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,118:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,118:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: | 4  | 5.30e-5
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,118:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: | 2.38e-3 | 1.18e-3 |
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,119:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,119:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: | 8  | 0 | 2.67e-5 | 3.70e-4 |
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,119:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,119:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: | 12 | 0 | 0 | 4.53e-4 |
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,119:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,120:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: | 16 | 0 | 0 | 3.94e-4 |
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,120:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,120:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: | 20 | 0 | 0 | 4.17e-4 |
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,120:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: All tests use the LS estimator with QPSK on the small factory.
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,121:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Analysis:
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,121:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: l
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,121:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Rayleigh is the easiest channel
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,123:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: , achieving zero BER above 8 dB. The frequency-flat fading with
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,123:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: independent realizations is well-suited to LS estimation with pilot-based interpolation.
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,123:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: l
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,124:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Rician (K=1)
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,125:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  reaches zero BER above 12 dB. The line-of-sight component helps, but the specular
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,125:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: path introduces estimation challenges at low SNR.
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,126:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: l
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,126:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: TR 38.901 UMi
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,130:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  is the hardest channel with a persistent BER floor of ~3-4 x 10^-4. The 3GPP
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,132:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: model includes realistic multipath, delay spread, and spatial correlation that LS cannot fully track.
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,133:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: This is the most realistic scenario for factory deployments.
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,133:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: l
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,138:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: The zero-BER results on Rayleigh/Rician suggest that the LDPC code is powerful enough to correct
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,139:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: residual errors when channel estimation is sufficiently accurate. The BER floor on TR 38.901 is due
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,140:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: to estimation limitations, not coding weakness.
   DEBUG: 2026-03-27 09:30:21,140:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 6. Factory Size Impact
